WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.060
Hey Crazies.

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:07.100
If you haven’t heard, light bends when it passes from something like air into water or glass.

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:10.640
The result is weird optical illusions like this.

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:15.000
In true Science Asylum style, we’re going to question everything.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.040
Let me show you how deep this rabbit hole goes.

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:25.240
This episode was made possible by generous supporters on Patreon.

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:28.400
Alright, let’s start with a basic line of reasoning.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:33.740
ATV clone is driving along the concrete when he suddenly encounters some mud.

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:37.960
As you can imagine, this noticeably slows the ATV,

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:39.900
but not all at once.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:44.340
The front right tire slows down before the other three tires.

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:48.080
That means there’s a speed imbalance on either side of the ATV,

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:50.480
which causes the ATV to turn.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:52.740
Its path becomes bent.

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:54.380
What’s this have to do with light?

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:56.420
I’m getting there. Chill out!

00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:59.060
Light seems to do the same thing.

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:02.900
Let’s say a drone is hovering in the air near my Scuba Clone.

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:07.940
The light that gets to his eyes isn’t going to be the light that went in a straight line.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:12.200
See, light travels at a different speed in the air than it does in water.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:16.200
Well, not really, but it’s useful to pretend that it does.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:20.740
Light certainly seems like it slows down, at least on our level of existence,

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:24.920
so it’s path is going to bend just like the ATV’s path did.

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:27.060
Just go with it.

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:31.820
The light that was originally headed for Scuba Clone’s eyes doesn’t make it there.

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:33.480
It gets bent away.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:39.780
We call this refraction, which comes from the Latin word “refractio” meaning “broken up."

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:43.740
The light that enters his eyes actually came from over here,

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:47.500
which means he doesn’t see the drone where it actually is.

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:48.740
Wait, what?!

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:50.640
Chill out and let me explain!

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:51.800
Aye yai yai.

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:57.560
We judge where things are by subconsciously assuming light travels along a straight line.

00:01:57.560 --> 00:01:59.500
It’s called line of sight.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:02.200
However, that’s not always the case.

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:08.160
So, when we see light coming at us from a certain angle, our brains assume it never bent.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:12.300
The light is interpreted as if it was always traveling in a straight line.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:15.320
Scuba Clone sees the drone over here.

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:19.100
This even explains the straw illusion at the beginning of the video.

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:23.860
If we look at this from above, the straw scatters light in all directions,

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:28.160
but the light headed directly toward our eyes doesn’t ever make it there.

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:31.960
The light that actually gets to our eyes came from this direction.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:35.680
But our brain thinks it came from a different direction.

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:38.520
We see the straw in a different place.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:40.700
Optical illusion explained!

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:42.900
How are you calculating all those angles though?

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:43.920
Snell’s law!

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:48.760
We’ve got the incoming angle on one side and the transmitted angle on the other.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:51.860
This “n” is called the refractive index.

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:56.780
That’s just a number you look up in a table somewhere, like this one on Engineering Toolbox.

00:02:56.780 --> 00:03:03.020
Each material has its own refractive index and how they compare controls the bending of light.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:07.200
A change in the incoming angle, means a change in the transmitted angle.

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:13.120
That refractive index is also related to the apparent speed of a light beam in that material.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:16.800
Speaking of speed, there might be a better way to look at this.

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:20.640
Let’s say our drone from earlier is being flown by Drone Clone

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:24.320
when it runs out of energy and begins falling into the lake.

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:29.300
Wanting to get there as fast as possible, his immediate thought is to run in a straight line.

00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:32.900
Unfortunately, when he reaches the water, it slows him down

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:35.000
and he can’t get there in time.

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:36.460
Goodbye drone!

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:40.560
A straight line might be the fastest path if you’re running through the air the whole time

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.320
or even the water the whole time.

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:45.160
But, if you have to travel through one and then the other,

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:47.360
a straight line is not the fastest.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:50.580
This straight line is the shortest distance,

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:53.140
but that doesn’t mean it’s the shortest time.

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:58.020
Drone Clone can actually get a faster time by staying out of the water a bit longer,

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:00.080
since he runs faster through the air.

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:05.160
But, that might give you the impression that he needs to stay in the air as long as possible,

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:06.920
which isn’t true either.

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:09.540
That path is too big of a distance.

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:12.400
The fastest path is somewhere in between.

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:15.440
That’s the one that balances distance with speed,

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:16.940
something like this.

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:19.360
A similar thing happens with light.

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:23.700
The path light takes is described by something called Fermat’s Principle,

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:28.900
which states that light will always take the path that requires the least amount of time.

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:30.420
Not the shortest distance.

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:31.740
The least time.

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:38.500
Light does what Drone Clone did without making any conscious decisions of any kind.

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:41.860
But why though?! Why do those two laws work that way?

00:04:41.860 --> 00:04:43.560
Quantum mechanics.

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:45.520
Here we go again.

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:47.880
Let’s ask ourselves a question:

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:52.620
What if light takes all possible paths, not just the least-time path?

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:57.740
I mean, we know a beam doesn’t do this, but maybe individual photons can?

00:04:57.740 --> 00:04:58.880
Somehow?

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:01.620
Alright, let’s take another look at Scuba Clone.

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:07.000
We know light travels from the drone to his eyes, but we don’t actually know the path.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:12.340
All we really know is that it started at the drone and ended at his eyes.

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:16.180
Each photon could have taken any path between those two points.

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:20.980
We might secretly hope it’s the least-time path, but we don’t know that for sure.

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:26.620
See, a photon's behavior is described by a wave, a wave of probability.

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:31.760
And, according to quantum mechanics, all of these paths contribute to that probability.

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:33.680
Yes, all of them!

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:36.780
We used this same technique in our video about mirrors.

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:39.760
If you’re getting uncomfortable, you should go watch that. I'll wait.

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:43.340
Ok, welcome back.

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:45.200
Rather than deal with wave mechanics,

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:50.020
we’re going to imagine the photon’s wave is a little arrow called a phase vector

00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:51.760
or phasor for short.

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:57.360
Over time, that arrow spins around at a pace given by the frequency of the photon.

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:02.860
Visible light frequencies are pretty high though, so this arrow is spinning around, like, crazy fast.

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:03.800
Fast fast!

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:08.419
Ultimately, each available path for the photon takes a different amount of time,

00:06:08.420 --> 00:06:12.380
so those spinning arrows end up in different orientations when they’re done.

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:16.900
To find the total probability that a photon will reach Scuba Clone’s eyes,

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:19.380
we need to add all these arrows together.

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:24.300
We do that by placing them all tail-to-head just like we’d add regular vectors.

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:28.040
But, the arrows caught up in the spirals tend to cancel themselves out,

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:32.060
leaving only a few paths to contribute in any noticeable way.

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:34.860
Those are the ones near the least-time path.

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:39.220
Fermat’s principle just comes from the probabilities of photons.

00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:41.140
It’s just probabilities.

00:06:41.140 --> 00:06:42.860
But that’s just a math trick, right?

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:46.020
The photon is really only taking the least-time path, right?!

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:49.540
Nope! All the paths matter and I can prove it.

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:54.840
Let’s keep things simple and say there’s a laser and a detector on opposite sides of the screen.

00:06:55.080 --> 00:07:00.000
Quantum tells us we must consider all paths between the laser and detector.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:04.220
It’s just that the probabilities of most paths cancel themselves out,

00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:07.600
leaving only the least-time path and the paths nearby.

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:12.760
But, what if we could slow some of those photons down by just the right amount.

00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:16.700
The amount of time each path takes looks something like this.

00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:18.860
The center path is the shortest time

00:07:18.860 --> 00:07:22.180
and they get progressively longer the farther they are from the center.

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:26.520
We know from earlier that a material like glass will slow the light down,

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:30.060
so we can adjust the timing if we just put some glass in the way.

00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:36.420
Adding glass along the short path means the time it takes a photon to travel that path is longer.

00:07:36.620 --> 00:07:38.660
You can see the graph has changed.

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:43.960
Now, what we want is for more of the paths to contribute to the probability.

00:07:44.180 --> 00:07:48.060
We want the timing for a bunch of the paths to be the same.

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:51.720
That way, they’ll add together instead of canceling each other out.

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:56.840
Each path needs a different time adjustment, which calls for a different amount of glass.

00:07:56.840 --> 00:07:59.320
We get a device that looks like this.

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:00.940
It’s just a lens.

00:08:01.100 --> 00:08:04.600
We just used quantum mechanics to explain how lenses work.

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:07.380
Photons have many paths available.

00:08:07.700 --> 00:08:11.460
A lens just changes the travel time for each of those paths.

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:14.600
That makes their probabilities add together differently

00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:17.840
and more paths contribute in a noticeable way.

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:20.640
That’s why this shape focuses light.

00:08:20.860 --> 00:08:22.240
Kind of cool, huh?

00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:27.500
We’ve seen that refraction, or the bending of light, can cause all sorts of optical illusions.

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:29.960
Like this famous one with the straw.

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:34.480
We could explain it with Snell’s law or even Fermat’s principle of least-time.

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:38.380
But why those laws work comes down to quantum mechanics.

00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:43.300
Light is made of photons and photons are a quantum mechanical object.

00:08:43.540 --> 00:08:45.980
Ultimately, they’re probabilistic.

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:50.840
Those probabilities help us explain refraction at multiple levels of complexity.

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:53.620
They can even explain how lenses work.

00:08:53.620 --> 00:08:55.480
It’s all really the same behavior.

00:08:55.640 --> 00:08:57.660
It’s all about probabilities.

00:08:57.820 --> 00:09:01.560
So, got any questions about refraction or just light in general?

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:03.040
Please ask in the comments.

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:04.820
Thanks for liking and sharing this video.

00:09:04.820 --> 00:09:07.400
Don’t forget to subscribe if you’d like to keep up with us.

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:11.020
And until next time, remember, it’s ok to be a little crazy.

00:09:12.460 --> 00:09:14.860
The featured comment comes from Tom Kerruish.

00:09:14.860 --> 00:09:16.760
Ka-rew-ish? Ka-rewsh?

00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:20.080
Why look up the refractive index when you can just calculate it?

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:21.320
Laziness?

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:22.960
Ok, Tom’s got a point though.

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:27.480
You can calculate it from the material’s EM field constants.

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:29.300
The equation looks like this.

00:09:29.300 --> 00:09:30.920
Anyway, thanks for watching!

